# plans/plan_dx9.md D9-A5 scene corpus: sprite_multitexture_quad
# Closes D9-90's own explicitly-named "known, explicitly-scoped-out gap": multi-texture
# SpriteBatch batching, i.e. a genuine FlushBatch()-on-texture-change mid-batch. Every earlier
# spritebatchmode scene either draws exactly one sprite, or (D9-93's sortmode scenes) 2+ sprites
# sharing the SAME texture object -- none has ever forced D3D9SpriteBatchRenderer::Draw() to see
# `currentTexture_ != nullptr && currentTexture_ != &texture` and actually flush+rebind mid-batch.
#
# Three NON-overlapping sprites at three separate positions, deliberately interleaved
# texture-A/texture-B/texture-A (not A-A-B or A-B-B, so the SECOND texture-A draw genuinely forces
# a SECOND texture rebind after the flush B triggered -- proving the rebind isn't a one-shot
# fluke). Since the sprites don't overlap, SpriteSortMode/blend order are irrelevant here (this
# scene deliberately does NOT use spritedraw='s sort-order machinery) -- the only thing under
# test is whether each destination rectangle shows the texture that was ACTUALLY bound for its
# own draw call, not a stale one left over from an earlier flush. texture (RED, 1x1) and texture2
# (BLUE, 1x1) are solid, maximally distinct colors specifically so a stale-binding bug is
# impossible to miss: a broken rebind would show BLUE (leaked from the middle draw) at the THIRD
# position instead of the expected RED.
width=256
height=256
profile=HiDef
clearcolor=0,0,0,255
spritebatchmode=true
texturewidth=1
textureheight=1
texturepixel=255,0,0,255
texture2=true
texture2width=1
texture2height=1
texture2pixel=0,0,255,255
spritedraw=20,108,40,40,255,255,255,255,0.0,0
spritedraw=108,108,40,40,255,255,255,255,0.0,1
spritedraw=196,108,40,40,255,255,255,255,0.0,0
